1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,560
It's an ordinary morning at the end of September.

2
00:00:05,017 --> 00:00:08,457
You wake up, make coffee, and check the news on your way to work.

3
00:00:08,917 --> 00:00:14,037
Something about a strange outbreak overseas. Hospitals somewhere are busy.

4
00:00:14,483 --> 00:00:17,283
You scroll past it. Everyone does.

5
00:00:17,750 --> 00:00:22,710
In the world of The Last of Us, that morning is the last normal one in human history.

6
00:00:23,150 --> 00:00:29,310
The game puts it in September 2013. The TV series moves it back to September 2003.

7
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:33,990
Either way, by the end of the week, the world you know is gone.

8
00:00:34,450 --> 00:00:37,570
Most videos about this story follow the heroes.

9
00:00:38,017 --> 00:00:39,697
This one follows you.

10
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:47,110
Not a smuggler. Not a soldier. Just an ordinary person with a job, a phone, and a fridge that's half empty.

11
00:00:47,550 --> 00:00:52,030
So let's live through it. Day one, week one, month one, year one.

12
00:00:52,483 --> 00:00:54,403
And then, twenty years later.

13
00:00:54,850 --> 00:01:00,450
Along the way, we'll look at what real science and real disasters tell us about how long you'd actually last.

14
00:01:02,267 --> 00:01:06,587
First, the bad news. The monster in this story is based on something real.

15
00:01:07,050 --> 00:01:12,410
In tropical forests, there is a fungus called Ophiocordyceps unilateralis.

16
00:01:12,850 --> 00:01:15,090
It infects carpenter ants.

17
00:01:15,550 --> 00:01:18,750
Once it's inside, the ant's behavior changes.

18
00:01:19,217 --> 00:01:26,577
It leaves its nest in the treetops, goes down to the low plants of the forest, and bites down hard on the vein of a leaf, on the underside.

19
00:01:27,017 --> 00:01:30,537
Then it stays there, locked in that bite, until it dies.

20
00:01:30,983 --> 00:01:36,503
After the ant dies, a stalk grows out of its head and releases spores onto the ground below.

21
00:01:36,950 --> 00:01:39,110
Right where other ants walk.

22
00:01:39,550 --> 00:01:45,390
Researchers really do call them "zombie ants." It's right in the title of the scientific paper.

23
00:01:45,850 --> 00:01:47,290
Now the good news.

24
00:01:47,750 --> 00:01:52,070
Most fungi can't survive inside us, because we're simply too hot.

25
00:01:52,517 --> 00:02:01,397
Our body sits at around thirty-seven degrees Celsius, and a study of more than four thousand fungal strains found that most of them can't grow at temperatures like that.

26
00:02:01,850 --> 00:02:07,130
Some researchers argue that our body heat works as a shield, protecting mammals from most fungi.

27
00:02:07,583 --> 00:02:16,623
The series opens with exactly this idea: a scientist on a TV show, warning that if the planet warms, fungi might adapt to higher temperatures.

28
00:02:17,083 --> 00:02:20,603
And then, one day, our temperature wouldn't protect us anymore.

29
00:02:21,050 --> 00:02:22,970
That part is fiction.

30
00:02:23,417 --> 00:02:26,697
But it's the kind of fiction that scientists actually discuss.

31
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:30,660
Back to you. Day one.

32
00:02:31,117 --> 00:02:34,477
By lunchtime, the news isn't about overseas anymore.

33
00:02:34,917 --> 00:02:41,237
In the series, the infection spreads through contaminated flour, and soon it is in many cities at once.

34
00:02:41,683 --> 00:02:45,843
In the game, it spreads through bites and through spores in the air.

35
00:02:46,283 --> 00:02:50,203
The infected don't shuffle like old movie zombies. The first ones run.

36
00:02:50,650 --> 00:02:54,730
And a bite is enough. Within hours or days, the person you knew is gone.

37
00:02:55,183 --> 00:02:57,983
Your first problem is not the infected.

38
00:02:58,450 --> 00:02:59,570
It's the phone.

39
00:03:00,017 --> 00:03:03,777
When something big happens, everyone calls everyone at the same moment.

40
00:03:04,217 --> 00:03:11,177
During the great blackout of August 2003, about fifty million people in the United States and Canada lost power in a few minutes.

41
00:03:11,617 --> 00:03:15,777
Cell networks were overwhelmed, and many calls simply didn't go through.

42
00:03:16,217 --> 00:03:18,297
So you can't reach your family.

43
00:03:18,750 --> 00:03:21,710
You get in the car. So does everyone else.

44
00:03:22,150 --> 00:03:29,590
In the series, the first night is chaos on the highways: crashes, fires, people running between stopped cars.

45
00:03:30,050 --> 00:03:32,450
That part is not hard to believe.

46
00:03:32,917 --> 00:03:38,437
Every hurricane evacuation shows the same thing: highways become parking lots.

47
00:03:38,883 --> 00:03:41,443
So most people on day one don't go far.

48
00:03:41,883 --> 00:03:46,043
They go home, lock the door, and wait for someone to tell them what to do.

49
00:03:47,833 --> 00:03:50,393
Here's something most people never think about.

50
00:03:50,850 --> 00:03:56,050
Almost everything in your house needs someone, somewhere, to keep working.

51
00:03:56,517 --> 00:04:02,037
The power plant needs workers. The water plant needs workers. The trucks need drivers.

52
00:04:02,483 --> 00:04:08,243
In the 2003 blackout, gas stations couldn't pump fuel, because the pumps run on electricity.

53
00:04:08,683 --> 00:04:13,643
In Cleveland, the pumping stations stopped, and about a million and a half people lost their water.

54
00:04:14,083 --> 00:04:18,323
In Cleveland and Detroit, people were told to boil their water.

55
00:04:18,783 --> 00:04:24,143
And that blackout lasted a day or two for most people, and up to four days in some places.

56
00:04:24,583 --> 00:04:26,743
Now imagine it never comes back.

57
00:04:27,183 --> 00:04:28,943
Food is the next problem.

58
00:04:29,383 --> 00:04:33,543
Supermarkets don't keep much stock. They live on constant deliveries.

59
00:04:33,983 --> 00:04:36,783
When the trucks stop, the shelves empty.

60
00:04:37,250 --> 00:04:41,970
And everyone remembers early 2020, when toilet paper disappeared in a weekend.

61
00:04:42,417 --> 00:04:46,097
So by the end of week one, you're counting cans.

62
00:04:46,550 --> 00:04:50,470
You've filled the bathtub with water, because someone online said to.

63
00:04:50,917 --> 00:04:54,917
That actually is official advice: store water before the taps fail.

64
00:04:55,383 --> 00:05:00,903
U.S. emergency agencies recommend at least one gallon of water per person, per day.

65
00:05:01,350 --> 00:05:06,950
Outside, the city is changing. Fewer cars. More sirens. Then, fewer sirens.

66
00:05:07,417 --> 00:05:10,617
And somewhere, the government is making a decision about you.

67
00:05:12,433 --> 00:05:16,433
In this world, the government doesn't try to save every city.

68
00:05:16,883 --> 00:05:19,603
It picks a few, and puts walls around them.

69
00:05:20,050 --> 00:05:23,250
These are the quarantine zones. QZs, for short.

70
00:05:23,717 --> 00:05:28,437
Boston. Pittsburgh. Seattle. Kansas City. A handful of others.

71
00:05:28,883 --> 00:05:32,323
Everything outside the walls is left to the infected.

72
00:05:32,783 --> 00:05:38,463
In the series, the agency that runs them is called FEDRA: the Federal Disaster Response Agency.

73
00:05:38,917 --> 00:05:42,437
If you live near one, you're lucky. Or you think you are.

74
00:05:42,883 --> 00:05:46,403
Getting in means lining up at a gate with hundreds of strangers.

75
00:05:46,850 --> 00:05:51,490
Soldiers check everyone for infection. Anyone who fails the test doesn't get in.

76
00:05:51,950 --> 00:05:56,990
And in this story, "doesn't get in" is a polite way of saying something much worse.

77
00:05:57,450 --> 00:05:59,930
Inside, your new life begins.

78
00:06:00,383 --> 00:06:03,823
You get a bed in an old building, often shared with strangers.

79
00:06:04,283 --> 00:06:07,883
There is a curfew. Break it, and you're in serious trouble.

80
00:06:08,350 --> 00:06:12,990
There are regular checks. Everyone is a suspect, all the time.

81
00:06:13,450 --> 00:06:15,610
Money is worthless now.

82
00:06:16,050 --> 00:06:18,530
What matters is ration cards.

83
00:06:18,983 --> 00:06:22,983
You work, and you get cards. You trade the cards for food.

84
00:06:23,450 --> 00:06:25,690
And the jobs are the ones nobody wants.

85
00:06:26,150 --> 00:06:31,830
In the series, people in the zone earn ration cards by working jobs for the zone.

86
00:06:32,283 --> 00:06:37,003
Hauling. Cleaning. Repairs. Whatever the soldiers need done.

87
00:06:37,450 --> 00:06:39,050
It's hard to complain, though.

88
00:06:39,517 --> 00:06:42,717
Every person inside the wall knows exactly what's outside it.

89
00:06:44,533 --> 00:06:48,133
By the end of the first year, the infected outside are changing too.

90
00:06:48,583 --> 00:06:53,543
In this world, the fungus doesn't just take over the brain. It keeps growing.

91
00:06:53,983 --> 00:06:59,023
The newly infected are called runners. Fast, aggressive, still almost human.

92
00:06:59,483 --> 00:07:04,363
After a while, they become stalkers: they hide, and they wait.

93
00:07:04,817 --> 00:07:07,457
Then comes the stage everyone fears.

94
00:07:07,917 --> 00:07:11,677
After a year or more, the fungus splits the face open, and covers the eyes.

95
00:07:12,117 --> 00:07:14,677
These are the clickers. They can't see anymore.

96
00:07:15,117 --> 00:07:18,797
So they hunt the way bats and dolphins do: with sound.

97
00:07:19,250 --> 00:07:22,050
They make a clicking noise, and listen to the echo.

98
00:07:22,517 --> 00:07:24,597
And that idea is real too.

99
00:07:25,050 --> 00:07:31,850
Some blind people actually learn to navigate this way, by clicking their tongue and listening to the echoes around them.

100
00:07:32,317 --> 00:07:37,277
Which means that in this world, the most important survival skill isn't shooting.

101
00:07:37,717 --> 00:07:39,237
It's being quiet.

102
00:07:39,683 --> 00:07:46,643
After many years, some infected become bloaters: huge, armored by layers of fungus, and very hard to kill.

103
00:07:47,083 --> 00:07:49,883
And when an infected finally dies, the fungus doesn't.

104
00:07:50,350 --> 00:07:55,710
In the game, it grows on the walls and releases clouds of spores. You'll want a gas mask.

105
00:07:56,150 --> 00:08:01,990
In the series, it mostly spreads through long tendrils underground, connecting the infected like a network.

106
00:08:02,450 --> 00:08:05,250
Remember the ant, biting the leaf?

107
00:08:05,717 --> 00:08:09,237
The real fungus doesn't care about the ant. It cares about the next ant.

108
00:08:09,683 --> 00:08:12,403
That's exactly what the fictional one does too.

109
00:08:14,200 --> 00:08:17,640
So what does a normal day look like for you, one year in?

110
00:08:18,083 --> 00:08:23,843
You wake up in the zone at dawn. There's no alarm clock, because there's barely any electricity.

111
00:08:24,283 --> 00:08:28,523
Breakfast is whatever the cards bought. Often the same thing every day.

112
00:08:28,983 --> 00:08:30,663
Then you report for work.

113
00:08:31,117 --> 00:08:36,397
Maybe you're on a cleaning crew. Maybe you repair walls. Maybe you're lucky, and you work in a kitchen.

114
00:08:36,850 --> 00:08:43,250
In the afternoon, there are lines. Lines for food, lines for water, lines for medicine.

115
00:08:43,717 --> 00:08:45,557
And there's a black market.

116
00:08:46,017 --> 00:08:53,457
In zones like these, people trade everything: pills, batteries, alcohol, cigarettes, information.

117
00:08:53,917 --> 00:08:57,117
Every disaster in real history has created one.

118
00:08:57,583 --> 00:09:06,223
Under wartime rationing in Britain, for example, there was an illegal trade in rationed goods, and people even had a name for the traders: spivs.

119
00:09:06,683 --> 00:09:08,683
In the evening, curfew.

120
00:09:09,150 --> 00:09:14,430
You go back to your room, eat, talk to whoever shares it with you, and go to sleep early.

121
00:09:14,883 --> 00:09:18,243
There's no TV, no internet, no new music.

122
00:09:18,683 --> 00:09:21,803
Just the sound of the wall, and the soldiers walking on it.

123
00:09:22,250 --> 00:09:23,930
And here's the strange thing.

124
00:09:24,383 --> 00:09:27,103
After a few months, it stops feeling strange.

125
00:09:27,550 --> 00:09:33,230
People get used to almost anything. Historians of every war and siege say the same thing.

126
00:09:33,683 --> 00:09:36,643
Life becomes routine. Even this.

127
00:09:38,433 --> 00:09:40,353
But not everyone stays inside.

128
00:09:40,817 --> 00:09:45,777
Some people hate the zones: the rules, the executions, the soldiers.

129
00:09:46,217 --> 00:09:51,337
In this story, there's a rebel group called the Fireflies, fighting the military government.

130
00:09:51,783 --> 00:09:55,703
Some people simply leave and try their luck outside.

131
00:09:56,150 --> 00:09:59,110
Out there, the infected are only one of the dangers.

132
00:09:59,550 --> 00:10:05,310
There are raiders and hunters: groups who survive by robbing, or killing, other survivors.

133
00:10:05,750 --> 00:10:08,470
And there are small communities that actually work.

134
00:10:08,917 --> 00:10:15,477
In the series, there's a town in Jackson, Wyoming, with walls, farms, horses, and electricity from a nearby dam.

135
00:10:15,917 --> 00:10:18,877
That's maybe the most realistic part of the whole story.

136
00:10:19,317 --> 00:10:24,677
When big systems collapse, people tend to rebuild in small groups, with whatever they have nearby.

137
00:10:25,117 --> 00:10:29,197
Water from a river. Power from a dam. Food from a field.

138
00:10:29,650 --> 00:10:34,130
Life starts to look less like 2013, and more like 1850.

139
00:10:35,933 --> 00:10:38,973
And in 1850, the biggest killer wasn't monsters.

140
00:10:39,417 --> 00:10:42,937
It was infection. The ordinary kind.

141
00:10:43,383 --> 00:10:47,303
In this world, nobody is making new antibiotics.

142
00:10:47,750 --> 00:10:52,390
Factories are gone, and the medicine that's left is expiring on the shelves.

143
00:10:52,850 --> 00:10:56,770
Before antibiotics, a small cut on your hand could kill you.

144
00:10:57,217 --> 00:11:02,977
Penicillin wasn't discovered until 1928, and it wasn't widely available until the 1940s.

145
00:11:03,417 --> 00:11:08,697
Before that, things like pneumonia and infected wounds killed huge numbers of people.

146
00:11:09,150 --> 00:11:15,230
In 1900, in the United States, about one in ten babies died before their first birthday.

147
00:11:15,683 --> 00:11:19,443
Childbirth was one of the most dangerous things a woman could do.

148
00:11:19,883 --> 00:11:22,923
And in the apocalypse, all of that comes back.

149
00:11:23,383 --> 00:11:27,543
No dentist. A bad tooth can become a deadly infection.

150
00:11:27,983 --> 00:11:29,983
No glasses, if yours break.

151
00:11:30,450 --> 00:11:36,130
No insulin for diabetics. No dialysis. No surgery that needs electricity.

152
00:11:36,583 --> 00:11:39,063
This is the part the stories usually skip.

153
00:11:39,517 --> 00:11:41,597
Most people wouldn't die from a bite.

154
00:11:42,050 --> 00:11:46,290
They'd die from a scratch on a rusty fence, or a fever in the winter.

155
00:11:48,100 --> 00:11:50,580
Meanwhile, the cities are going green.

156
00:11:51,017 --> 00:11:53,897
And here, we actually know a lot about what would happen.

157
00:11:54,350 --> 00:12:02,750
Without pumps, the New York subway would start to flood, because water constantly leaks into its tunnels and has to be pumped out every day.

158
00:12:03,217 --> 00:12:10,657
Without people, grass breaks through the asphalt. Trees grow on rooftops. Rust eats the bridges.

159
00:12:11,117 --> 00:12:12,717
We have a real example.

160
00:12:13,183 --> 00:12:19,343
After the Chernobyl nuclear disaster in 1986, people abandoned a huge area around the plant.

161
00:12:19,783 --> 00:12:29,143
Decades later, a long-term census found elk, deer and wild boar as abundant as in nearby nature reserves, and wolves more than seven times more numerous.

162
00:12:29,583 --> 00:12:34,703
Even Przewalski's horses live there now, though they were released on purpose, in 1998.

163
00:12:35,150 --> 00:12:44,510
The researchers don't say radiation is good for wildlife. They say the effect of human presence, with hunting, farming and forestry, is a lot worse.

164
00:12:44,950 --> 00:12:52,470
In the world of The Last of Us, you see the same thing: giraffes walking through a ruined city, deer in the streets.

165
00:12:52,917 --> 00:12:56,757
It's beautiful. And it's a reminder of how fast the world forgets us.

166
00:12:58,567 --> 00:13:01,047
Now, twenty years have passed.

167
00:13:01,483 --> 00:13:05,483
In the game, it's 2033. In the series, 2023.

168
00:13:05,950 --> 00:13:08,190
If you survived, you're a different person.

169
00:13:08,650 --> 00:13:13,770
You know how to stay quiet. You know how to stretch a can of beans for two days.

170
00:13:14,217 --> 00:13:18,377
You know which streets are safe, and which buildings you never, ever enter.

171
00:13:18,817 --> 00:13:21,857
And there's a new generation who never knew the old world.

172
00:13:22,317 --> 00:13:24,877
Kids who have never seen a working airplane.

173
00:13:25,317 --> 00:13:30,677
Who have never seen a movie, or eaten ice cream, or ridden in a car on a highway.

174
00:13:31,117 --> 00:13:35,117
For them, the stories adults tell about the old world sound like fairy tales.

175
00:13:35,583 --> 00:13:42,623
Cities that glowed all night. Food that arrived on trucks. A phone that could talk to anyone on Earth.

176
00:13:43,083 --> 00:13:45,803
And slowly, knowledge disappears.

177
00:13:46,250 --> 00:13:52,170
Who still knows how to make penicillin? How to fix a power line? How to build an engine?

178
00:13:52,617 --> 00:13:57,177
Every year, the people who remember die, and some of that knowledge dies with them.

179
00:13:57,617 --> 00:13:59,697
Historians have seen this before.

180
00:14:00,150 --> 00:14:06,710
After the Western Roman Empire fell, many building skills disappeared from parts of Western Europe for centuries.

181
00:14:07,150 --> 00:14:14,910
In Britain, for example, mass-produced pottery disappeared after the Romans left, and for centuries almost nobody built in stone.

182
00:14:15,350 --> 00:14:18,790
A civilization doesn't need to be destroyed to go backwards.

183
00:14:19,250 --> 00:14:21,090
It just needs to stop teaching.

184
00:14:22,900 --> 00:14:24,900
So, would you survive?

185
00:14:25,350 --> 00:14:28,070
Honestly? Probably not the first week.

186
00:14:28,517 --> 00:14:32,597
Most people in a disaster this fast don't die because they're weak or stupid.

187
00:14:33,050 --> 00:14:36,810
They die because they're in the wrong place, at the wrong time.

188
00:14:37,250 --> 00:14:42,130
But if you made it into a zone, and you kept your head down, and you never got a cut infected...

189
00:14:42,583 --> 00:14:46,983
Then your life would look a lot like the lives of most people in human history.

190
00:14:47,450 --> 00:14:53,050
Hard work. Simple food. Short nights. Little news from the outside world.

191
00:14:53,517 --> 00:14:56,317
Rules made by people you never voted for.

192
00:14:56,783 --> 00:14:59,183
And a small group of people you trust.

193
00:14:59,650 --> 00:15:02,690
In a way, that's what makes The Last of Us frightening.

194
00:15:03,150 --> 00:15:05,310
It's not that the world becomes something new.

195
00:15:05,750 --> 00:15:09,830
It's that the world goes back to something very, very old.

196
00:15:10,283 --> 00:15:13,723
And here's the last thing the real fungus can teach us.

197
00:15:14,183 --> 00:15:18,823
The zombie ant fungus works because the ant stops behaving like part of its colony.

198
00:15:19,283 --> 00:15:21,763
It leaves the others, and walks away alone.

199
00:15:22,217 --> 00:15:25,577
In the story, the fungus never wins because it's smart.

200
00:15:26,017 --> 00:15:29,457
It wins every time people stop trusting each other.

201
00:15:29,917 --> 00:15:32,397
Maybe that's the real survival skill.

202
00:15:32,850 --> 00:15:35,490
Not the gun. Not the gas mask.

203
00:15:35,950 --> 00:15:36,990
Each other.

204
00:15:37,450 --> 00:15:41,610
If you liked this, tell me in the comments: how long do you think you'd last?

205
00:15:42,050 --> 00:15:45,410
And which "ordinary life" should we live through next?
